{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 4, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 1.0

    ,
      "why": "Only family that can plausibly meet the system batch constraint. The control tick issues 32 requests at once and all must finish within 0.44 us, implying an effective throughput of ~73 Msps. Iterative has cycles/result = N+3 >= 15 for N>=12 (needed for 2^-12 accuracy), requiring Fmax > 1 GHz, which is infeasible on Artix-7. unrolled_k reduces cycles/result to ceil(N/k)+3; with N>=12 and k>=6, this is 5 cycles, needing Fmax >= 363 MHz. The ranges cover the trade-off between area (W, N, k, guards) and accuracy/throughput, focusing on N=12..16 and k=6..8 where cycles/result is minimal, while allowing wider exploration to map the Pareto front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec's system constraint sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 is the binding one: 32 requests in 0.44 us requires ~73 Msps effective throughput. Iterative cannot achieve this because its cycles/result is at least N+3, and N must be >=12 for the 2^-12 accuracy constraint, giving >=15 cycles/result and requiring >1 GHz Fmax. unrolled_k can reduce cycles/result by chaining k micro-rotations per cycle. With N=12..16 and k=6..8, cycles/result is 5, requiring Fmax >= 363 MHz, which is challenging but plausible. We allocate the entire 100-eval budget to unrolled_k, searching data_width 12..20, n_iter 12..20, angle_guard -2..4, frac_guard 0..4, rounding trunc/round, and k 4..8. This covers the feasible region for accuracy and area while letting Optuna focus on the minimal-area designs that meet all constraints."
}

